  "status": "published",
  "title": "U.S. Officials Weighed a Cap on Kim’s Weapons Tests. North Korea Is Expanding Its Enrichment Plant.",
  "deck": "A testing limit could slow some weapons development. Whether it would also curb production of nuclear material is a separate question.",
  "body": [
    "Days before The Wall Street Journal reported that U.S. officials had discussed caps on North Korean nuclear and missile testing, the United Nations nuclear watchdog reported an expansion at the country’s Yongbyon uranium-enrichment plant. The two developments raise a question any Trump–Kim agreement would have to answer. What could North Korea still build while its weapons tests were on hold?",
    "The Journal, citing people familiar with the discussions, reported that U.S. officials considered seeking testing limits and issuing a statement that would omit a demand to dismantle North Korea’s nuclear program. An administration official disputed that efforts had been made to broker a meeting. No agreement has been publicly confirmed. In September, a White House official told Yonhap that President Donald Trump remained open to dialogue without preconditions and that U.S. policy had not changed.",
    "North Korea conducted no nuclear explosive tests in 2018. In January 2019, the Congressional Research Service reported that the country was still producing fissile material for weapons. A pause in testing could limit opportunities to validate new designs and reduce tensions. The earlier pause shows how production can continue in parallel.",
    "At the Hanoi summit in February 2019, North Korean officials said they offered to shut down production facilities at Yongbyon in exchange for sanctions relief. The talks ended without an agreement. U.S. officials had identified disclosure of enrichment sites beyond Yongbyon as a key issue for negotiations.",
    "On November 18, International Atomic Energy Agency Director General Rafael Grossi said North Korea had built an extension to its original enrichment plant at Yongbyon. The agency has had no on-site access since North Korea expelled its inspectors in 2009 and relies mainly on satellite imagery. SIPRI estimated that North Korea had about 60 warheads as of January 2026.",
    "Trump and South Korean President Lee Jae Myung have reaffirmed their commitment to North Korea’s complete denuclearization. Kim pledged in February to expand his arsenal and tied better relations to Washington accepting North Korea’s nuclear status. The practical questions are whether any agreement would cover production as well as testing, how compliance would be verified, and what Washington would offer in return."
  ],
